My thoughts regarding the 22#super[nd] division of the gospel of Luke.

// Exodus 13:3
_Remember the day in which you went out of Egypt, out of the house of bondage; for
by the strength of the hand of the LORD brought you out of this place. No leavened
bread shall be eaten._ \
Exodus 13:3

== Christ knew suffering was inevitable

This passage begins by introducing the scribes desire to murder the Christ.
Judas Iscariot decides to help them, and betray his master. Christ understands
that this betrayal is coming, which we can infer because he says this during
the Passover meal: _the hand of my betrayer is with me on the table_.

That he understands his fate is clear in several other statements he makes during
his final hours. As dinner begins, Christ says that he longed to eat the passover
with his disciples _before I suffer_. He understood that the hour
of his suffering was drawing close.

That he was aware the scripture would be fulfilled in him is evident by the remark
after his teaching: _this which is written must still be accomplished in me:
and he was numbered among the transgressors. For the things concerning me have an
end._ Twice he says (in different ways) that the scriptures would be fulfilled
in him. First by the word accomplished. Then by the word end. That which is written
and the things concerning him are one and the same. The repitition seems to be a
way of emphasis --- the scriptures will be fulfilled. And what is the specific
topic to be fulfilled? That he is to be considered, judged, and condemned as a
transgressor.

Rather than avoid this fate, he makes certain it will happen. He
instructs his disciples to equip themselves with weapons. This is
something them have seemingly never had need of. As he is arrested, the
disciples use their weapons to defend their master.  Does he encourage
their use of weapons? I do not think so, because imemdiately after the
servant of the high priest (the person arresting him) looses an ear to
a sword, Christ heals him. And afterwards he speaks to his followers:
_permit this_. This is his response to the earlier question:
_Lord, shall we strike him with the sword?_ Seemingly not. He is
prepared to be arrested.

As he is betrayed, Judas Iscariot draws near to kiss his master. Before the
rest of the disciples understand what the approaching multitude means,
Christ asks Judas Iscariot _are you betraying the Son of Man with a kiss?_
He identifies the betrayal immediately.

Christ's knowledge of what is coming is revealed in his prayer.
To the Father, he prays: _if it is not your will, take this cup from me;
nevertheless, not my will but yours, be done_. He does not desire the
suffering, but he is ready to endure it because he knows it is his Father's
will. Does he equate what is written in the scripture to the Father's will?
Not necessarily I do not think. I would argue no because he asks the Father
to take this cup away --- something that would be unusual, seeing as
what was written clearly testifies that the Son of Man must suffer. This
may be indication that the Father's will may superscede that which is
written in the scripture. I am not entirely sure what to make of this yet.

As he breaks the bread during passover, he tells his disciples it is
his _body, which is given for you_. Likewise, the wine he calls
his _blood, which is shed for you_. Both statements are indicative
of the events to come. These are the same event, refered to previously:
_until *it* is fulfilled in the kingdom of God_.

Again during dinner, after exposing that his betrayer was among his closest
friends, he declares that even his betrayal is within the Father's plan:
_truly the Son of Man goes as it has been determined_. Who is the
determiner, but the Father? What is the course of the Son of Man? Suffering,
and death.

The Son of Man understands he is going to be betrayed. This is revealed five
distinct ways (that I can identify). Yet he is determined that his Father's
will would preside. This was a period of extreme temptation, which will be brought
out next.

== Christ was tempted

That he is being tempted in these final hours is clear by his instruction
to his disciples: _Pray, that you enter not into temptation_ and
_Why do you sleep? Rise and pray, lest you enter into temptation_.
Temptation was so great no one could afford the luxury of sleep at
this time. Prayer was necessary to avoid the test to come. And although
Christ prays for Peter, his boldest disciple is still sifted as wheat.

The temptation is clearly to avoid the suffering to come. He understands the extreme
level of suffering he is about to endure, as was shown above. So he prays earnestly
to the Father that he would _take this cup away_. This phrase is a way of
asking the Father to extract him from the coming suffering. Rather than giving into
his desire to avoid pain, he submits to his Father's will. This is a fantastic
example of obedience.

